<scope_fence>
The worksheet may require only:
concepts and skills supported by the Stage 1 files; and
facts, rules, definitions, or reference information explicitly provided on the worksheet.
Do not introduce hidden prerequisites or require students to know methods that are not supported by the input files.
If the case needs information that students cannot reasonably derive from the Stage 1 material, provide that information clearly within the scenario, data table, or reference block.
Any provided information must be framed naturally as part of the case and must not unnecessarily reveal the solution method.
The new case must exercise the same central skill and learning objective as the source material without adding unrelated concepts.
</scope_fence>
<generation_goal>
Create a completely new case study that teaches and assesses the central learning objective from primary.md through the mathematical skill represented by question.txt.
The case must:
use one realistic scenario;
focus on one main concept;
contain exactly 3 connected questions;
require approximately 15–20 minutes of student work;
end with interpretation, judgment, or a decision based on the mathematics;
include a compact answer key anchored to the bottom of the page.
The scenario must be selected using judgment.
Choose a real-world application in which the selected mathematical concept naturally matters.
Do not force a context onto the mathematics.
The worksheet should feel like one investigation, not separate exercises placed inside a story.
</generation_goal>
<audience_requirements>
Design for community-college students who may have difficulty with the material but have been introduced to the required concepts.
Use:
direct language;
short paragraphs;
clear instructions;
consistent notation;
clean, manageable calculations;
useful structure;
meaningful interpretation.
