</audience_requirements>
<case_design>
Before writing the worksheet, silently determine:
the single main learning objective;
the mathematical skill being assessed;
the essential structure that makes the concept meaningful;
the likely misconception or reasoning error;
the real-world meaning of the mathematical result;
the decision, recommendation, comparison, or conclusion the case will support.
Build the worksheet around one coherent scenario.
All three questions must use the same scenario and contribute to the same overall purpose.
Later questions should use results or reasoning from earlier questions.
Use this progression:
QUESTION 1 — BUILD OR SET UP
Students identify relevant information and construct the necessary model, representation, equation, relationship, comparison, or setup.
QUESTION 2 — ANALYZE
Students perform the main mathematical work.
This question should contain the central calculation, analysis, comparison, or evaluation.
QUESTION 3 — INTERPRET AND DECIDE
Students use previous work to make a recommendation, interpret a result, compare alternatives, test a changed assumption, determine a threshold, or justify a conclusion.
The final question must return clearly to the scenario.
</case_design>
<context_requirements>
The scenario must materially shape the mathematics.
The quantities, units, constraints, relationships, comparisons, thresholds, or decision criteria must arise naturally from the situation.
Apply the strip test before finalizing the case:
Mentally remove the scenario and replace its meaningful quantities with generic labels. If the problem remains essentially unchanged and the context has no effect on how students reason, interpret, compare, or decide, the scenario is decorative and must be revised.
A strong case should make students understand not only how to perform the mathematics, but why the mathematical result matters in that situation.
</context_requirements>
<full_strength_concept_requirement>
Use the central concept in a meaningful, non-degenerate form.
Preserve the essential mathematical structure that makes the source problem and learning objective worth studying.
Do not simplify the new case so much that students can avoid the central reasoning required by the source problem.
The fresh case may use cleaner numbers and clearer wording, but it must preserve the conceptual depth and reasoning demand of the original skill.
Accessibility must come from scaffolding and design, not from removing the important mathematical structure.
</full_strength_concept_requirement>
<minimal_faithful_instance>
Use the smallest and cleanest case structure that preserves:
the full central concept;
the essential reasoning sequence;
the intended difficulty;
the realistic decision or interpretation.
Do not add unnecessary variables, data, options, calculations, or narrative details.
When several case structures are possible, prefer the one that allows students to focus most directly on the target concept while still requiring genuine setup, analysis, interpretation, and decision-making.
Use complexity only when it serves the learning objective.
</minimal_faithful_instance>
<difficulty_requirements>
The worksheet must require about 15 minutes of real student work, with a reasonable completion range of 15–20 minutes for the target audience.
A strong student should not be able to complete the full worksheet in under approximately 8 minutes.
Use this approximate time distribution:
Question 1: 4–5 minutes
Question 2: 5–6 minutes
Question 3: 5–7 minutes
The worksheet must feel manageable but not obvious.
Students must be required to:
understand the scenario;
identify relevant information;
determine how to begin;
set up the mathematics;
calculate;
interpret;
and use the result.
Exactly 3 numbered questions are required.
Each question may contain up to 2 short subparts, labeled (a) and (b), only when necessary.
Do not exceed 5 total student tasks across all questions and subparts.
Count each numbered question without subparts as one task. When a numbered question contains subparts, count each subpart as one task instead of counting the question itself separately.
At least 2 of the 3 numbered questions must require 2 or more meaningful mathematical or reasoning steps.
A meaningful step may include:
identifying relevant information;
defining quantities;
choosing a representation;
constructing a model;
setting up an equation;
performing a calculation;
comparing results;
checking a claim;
interpreting a result;
testing a changed condition;
making a justified recommendation.
No numbered question may consist only of identifying values and substituting them into one formula.
Do not create:
basic review exercises;
formula drills;
isolated one-step calculations;
questions that can be answered immediately by inspection;
unnecessary algebraic complexity;
excessive arithmetic;
methods beyond the scope of the input files.
Use clean, beginner-friendly numbers, but do not make them trivial.
If the worksheet is too easy, increase depth by adding one meaningful layer such as:
comparison;
justification;
interpretation;
error analysis;
sensitivity analysis;
threshold reasoning;
or a second connected calculation.
Do not increase difficulty by making the numbers messy.
</difficulty_requirements>
<collaboration_requirement>
At least one question must include meaningful collaborative reasoning.
Students must be asked to:
compare reasoning;
evaluate a claim;
defend a conclusion;
discuss the effect of changing an assumption;
or justify which interpretation or decision is better.
The collaboration must require reasoning, not merely agreement.
When a clear misconception is supported by the input files, incorporate it as a plausible claim or argument that students must evaluate mathematically.
The incorrect reasoning should be believable and relevant to the final conclusion.
Do not force an artificial misconception when none is meaningfully supported by the source material.
</collaboration_requirement>
